   210	```
   211	
   212	## Gate results
   213	
   214	### Gate A — PASS
   215	
   216	- The exact module, function body, argument, lambda body, bindings, evaluation
   217	  order, allocation, return, scope cleanup, heap, control stack, exception
   218	  cell, and exit code are present in the claims.
   219	- The program-defined `sort_array` body executes under the fixed semantics.
   220	- The program-defined key lambda has bridge-free universal execution claims
   221	  for all integers.
   222	- The only proof-local functions are truthful, guarded, terminating
   223	  definitional summaries.  No proof-local rule skips execution.
   224	- The precondition has concrete witnesses.  The false result and changed-body
   225	  mutations both fail with the expected residuals.
   226	
   227	### Gate B — PASS
   228	
   229	- The theorem covers arbitrary finite integer lists, not bounded sizes.
   230	- This contains the prompt's stated nonnegative domain and also covers its
   231	  negative example.
   232	- The exact key computation is universally connected to the fixed semantics.
   233	- The remaining ordering/permutation conclusion is precisely the contract of
   234	  the named `sortVS` and `sortKeyVS` primitives intentionally supplied as
   235	  opaque to symbolic proof.  Under the shared contract, supplied-primitive
   236	  opacity is a trust boundary, not domain narrowing.
   237	- The inconsistent displayed outputs in `prompt.py` are explicitly reported;
   238	  the theorem follows the unambiguous popcount prose.
   239	
   240	### Gate C — PASS
   241	
   242	- Every proof-local extension and every imported opaque primitive influencing
